A||||24||scaredfiero||Please read...I need help, what should I do? Doctors, anyone???||||9||
Z||000000||scaredfiero||11-11-2001||07:07 AM||||I have been a member of Pennocks for several years, I have met many people and made good friendships through here. I am not using my real id becuase I do not want those who know me to worry.<P>This is long.<P>A couple of months ago, a good friend told me she had Leukemia. She has been battling it since this spring with bone marrow transplant and chemo. The doctors took tests last week and found that the leukemia has spread through her body and she needs another transplant. The doctors don't know how much time she has, they can't even guarantee she will get to see the end of the year unless she has the transplant. She has no health insurance, and not enough to pay for the transplant. A good friend of hers, has come into millions of dollars. He is the one who payed for her first transplant, with cash. She didn't want him to pay for the first, but he insisted and wouldn't allow her to refuse. This time, she is not telling him, because she doesn't want him or anyone to trouble themselves for her. Her family has abandoned her and basically cut her off from them, same with her brother. She and her brother live with a good family friend, who she also says would pay for her transplant, but she doesn't want them to trouble over her, so she doesn't tell them whats going on.<BR>I am the only person she has told. Her brother also knows, and is the reason her friend found out the first time. It has been a week, and apparently her brother hasn't told anyone about this yet.<P>She says she needs $5,000 as the downpayment to get things rolling and have the transplant performed. She has a form of Luekemia with a higher sucess and cure rate than most types. <BR>I feel this is almost a sucide if she isn't persuing all options to fight this. She has been extremely emotional about this, but she won't let others fuss over her by helping with money, so she doesn't tell them. I don't have the money, and it would be hard to make her accept it if I did.<P>What should I do? Where do I go? Is there anyplace people turn to when they need medical treatment and don't have the funds? I've always been very healthy, and always had healther insurance, so this is all new to me. <BR>Anyone?????????||||reg||9
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||11-11-2001||07:33 AM||||I'm verry sorry to hear about your friend.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>She shouldn't be ashamed to ask the people with the funds to help her. They probably really <B>want</B> to help her. There isn't anything better to spend money on then trying to save a friend's life. If it bothers her so much, make an arrangement that if she makes it through all of this, she will pay them back.<P>Try to talk it out of her mind that's she's bothering people by asking for help. She isn't. They want one thing and one thing only: to see her survive. And they have the means to help her. If she's really concerned about the wellbeing of her friends, ask her how she thinks they will feel if she's gone. Tell her it's unfair that she doesn't give her friends a chance to save her. Money comes and goes. A lost friend is gone forever.||||reg||1
Z||000002||maryjane||11-11-2001||07:52 AM||||I'm assuming you are NOT from Canada, since they have a National Health Program. Your first obligation is to convince her that her life is worth continuing. She may have gotten tired of fighting the pain and given up. (It's a tough road, I went thru it a few weeks ago, when we had exceeded Jane's ins coverage. Got a lot of help from PFF members, and I think we're OK now. )First, contact Leukemia Foundation. (I think that is what it is called). They will help find funding/grants. Contact MAJOR teaching hospitals. They often have programs to do this sort of thing. Write, call & email your state, local, and fed representatives. They WILL respond, they have more assets to set in motion than you do, but there are things that you can do to help. (First Lady Laura Bush called me personally.)Get her declared medically disabled, apply for medicaid/SSI. All states have a certain amt of federal funding for indigent health care, contributed to by Medicaid/medicare. It's usually on a County type deal. Larger population centers have more funding than your small counties. These people are hard to work with, but they can be pushed into action if you are persistant. <BR>I still have most of the WWW links to help with this. Email me : janedon1@earthlink.net.<BR>I don't need your name or city, just general area of the country. I can assure anonominity.(sp) Pray, it helps. (RayB,Johnny, please stay out of this one) I'll get started right after breakfast. Good luck.<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||TRiAD||11-11-2001||08:42 AM||||First; I am VERY sorry for the situation, and will be praying you her, you, and everyone involved. <P>But, I am a firm believer in "do whatever it takes" in situations like these...<P>With that in mind...<P>I say tell her friends about this. With or without her permission. She is very ill, and may not be able to make the right decisions right now. Tell her friend she's staying with, tell the friend who came into money, tell her family, tell <I>everyone</I>. <P>I would contact St. Jude's, and see if they know anyone who could help someone her age (St. Jude's offers free help for children). <P>All of this said (and take this with a HUGE grain of salt); the last time I ran into this (and I've seen it a few times myself) it was not cancer, but a suicide attempt. The person I knew "refused to tell anyone" because she wasn't really sick. She was calling for help and wanted to know who really cared. I did, and took my own advice, and she "got caught". She found out who really cared; we all did; but needed some serious therapy before she could speak to me again. <BR><FONT COLOR="blue">I'm sure that's not the case here</FONT>, <BR>but either way, you can see where it's important, if you really care about her, to tell...everyone...immediately. <P>Our prayers are with you...||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||11-11-2001||08:47 AM||||Triad's right, as is Cliff. Do whatever it takes. We'll help however we can. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||scaredfiero||11-11-2001||08:52 AM||||I want to thank everyone so far. Don, you have mail. <P>She just told me this last night after lying about it for a week, telling me the doctors said she didn't need a transplant, but I finally got it out of her last night. She was kind of mad that I dug until she finally spilled it. But, I would rather lose a friendship than a friend.<P>I am debating telling her "parents" her friend with money, etc. If I do, she may push me away, and make it that much harder for me to help her. If I don't she may not be around to help at all.<P>TRiAD: I've been in one of your situations before. This time, I'm not going to miss the signals. I lost a friend to suicide, I will not stand by and watch another friend allow the system to do it for them.<P><B>She was calling for help and wanted to know who really cared. </B> I am wondering if her situation is similar.||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroBUZZ||11-11-2001||08:58 AM||||Very sorry to hear about your friend.  Another approach may be to talk to the friend who funded the first go round, if you believe he is willing and able to help.<P>He could do it anonimously (through a hospital or lawyer) if that is necessary.  In the end it will be up to the lady in question as much of the success in these things is due to the will to live/succeed.<P>All the best, don't just give up and let it ride.  Don's advise is good and he has obviously been down this road before.<P>Gary||||reg||9
Z||000007||scaredfiero||11-12-2001||01:12 PM||||There are new developments in this story. I am at work right now, but tonight will post and ask what steps I should consider next.<P>I want to thank everyone with this so far. Don and Jane, you have helped tremendously, thank you.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Jaygee79||11-12-2001||02:14 PM||||if it were me i would do anything I could possibly do to help her.  I would tell the friend and see if they could pay it without her knowing.  after its paid for she cant refuse it.  its her life thats on the line, i personally would try to make sure that she gets to keep it.  but thats JMHO.  Maryjane had some great suggestions too.  I'll also pray for her and for you.  Please let us know what happens.  <p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 11-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||fogglethorpe||11-12-2001||05:23 PM||||I'm sorry our friend is ill...<P>This may sound strange, but it may be a good idea. Hold a beef-n-beer bash, and invite the public, telling them up front that it is a charity event for your friend. Try to get food and a hall donated, and charge by the head, with all proceeds going to her medical fund.<BR>This is do-able...as long as you can assure people it's legitimate, it will come together.<BR>A touchy part of this would be deciding to either A) Go against her wishes and make it public, or B) Get her permission. You will have to make that decision.<BR>If this event happens, she will see the outpouring from other people, and will be encouraged enough to want to fight this.<BR>I hope this helps...best wishes||||reg||1
Z||000010||scaredfiero||11-17-2001||11:04 PM||||I had typed up a whole update typed up while at work the other day, but hotmail lost it. Haven't had much time to type again since.<P>I tried to get her to let me help with her knowledge about it, but she got PISSED. So, I told her I would back off like she asked. I have since been speaking "in secret" with her brother. It turns out he does care much more than she had let me believe(she has it in her mind no-one cares for her and that she doesn't deserve anything, she couldn't be more wrong). I told her brother that her friend with money didn't know(it turns out she had lied to her brother and told him she had told her friend, but she hadn't). He stopped where he was and called her friend and let him know what was up.<P>Her condition is getting worse, she is getting sicker and is not getting better. Her brother says she needs the transplant before the end of the year(actually almost, right now). He also says that people at her work, at his work, at her college, and a few other "groups" of people she knows are secretly putting money together. Her brother tells me the funding should arrive in time for a transplant to take place. Her brother will be the donor again. He says she is still against anyone helping, but he says she is in the very beginning stages of appearing to accept help soon. I am so glad, I just hope she gets the help she needs before it is too late. Turns out she also needs a lot more than $5000 to get things moving, but it appears money could be taken care of. One of the "groups" donating for her has LOTS. I can't tell more in detail, I don't think its a good idea right now, but I will let you know how this goes.||||reg||1
Z||000011||maryjane||11-17-2001||11:10 PM||||Sorry to hear she isn't getting better but the news about her brother & others sounds encouraging. Her getting mad sounds familar, but I doubt it is indicative of her real feeelings. Are you hanging in there ok? God Bless<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000012||scaredfiero||11-18-2001||01:20 AM||||I've been doing a lot better this week. Its really terrible what she has been through. She was beaten by her parents while growing up all the way until she was kicked out when she turned 18. She has dated guys who beat her. Her last boyfriend left her because she learned she was sick with Leukemia. Because of her Leukemia, he left her! I met her after all of this stuff, and its probably a good thing, cause I know of a few guys who wouldn't be walking right now if I had known them at the time. She is in much better care and around people who truly care about her now. But, in her mind, she isn't worth anything, no-one cares about her, and she believes she deserved getting hit and getting Leukemia. Its been a struggle for me and others to see her believe this about herself.<P>She never mentioned her brother wanting to help, and gave the impression he didn't care. One day I decided it was time to give him a piece of my mind. We got to talking, and it turned out I was all wrong about him. I ended up giving him a lot of information he didn't know. As it turned out she had told only me, she trusted me and no-one else. I felt bad telling him her secrets, but I told myself it was for the best, and I truly believe I have made the correct decisions thus far. If I hadn't told him, money wouldn't be in the process of being collected, people wouldn't be taking care of issues, etc. <BR>She is getting weaker, she is battling a cold or something, and isn't doing real well. I'm worried, but her brother says they will have the money in time for a transplant. I really hope so. Her brother was the donor for the first transplant, and when her parents found out he was going to be the donor the first time, they told her brother if he went through with it, he shouldn't plan on coming back home. Her brother went through with it, and now lives with the family friend with her. She never did anything to bring her parents down on her, she just got the luck of the draw for $hitty parents. It makes me realize how fortunate I really am to have parents who always have and always will, care for me.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Jay||11-18-2001||10:07 AM||||I know how you feel.  My cousin died of Leukimea a few years ago.  In order to raise money for the donor test we did the fund raising thing.  Pig roast with the whole community invited.  We got companies to donate raffle items and prizes.  Everything was for sale and we raised alot of money.  Unfortunately he didn't make it, no donor was found, but it was the largest turnout ever to be tested, and all these results are now on file.  Your friend is one up.  She already has a match.  Do whatever you have to do.  You don't want to live the rest of your life wondering if you made the right decision.  At all costs get her help both medical and emotional.||||reg||1
Z||000014||falconhulk||11-19-2001||02:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by scaredfiero:<BR><B>I've been doing a lot better this week. Its really terrible what she has been through. She was beaten by her parents while growing up all the way until she was kicked out when she turned 18. She has dated guys who beat her. Her last boyfriend left her because she learned she was sick with Leukemia. Because of her Leukemia, he left her! I met her after all of this stuff, and its probably a good thing, cause I know of a few guys who wouldn't be walking right now if I had known them at the time. She is in much better care and around people who truly care about her now. But, in her mind, she isn't worth anything, no-one cares about her, and she believes she deserved getting hit and getting Leukemia. Its been a struggle for me and others to see her believe this about herself.<P>She never mentioned her brother wanting to help, and gave the impression he didn't care. One day I decided it was time to give him a piece of my mind. We got to talking, and it turned out I was all wrong about him. I ended up giving him a lot of information he didn't know. As it turned out she had told only me, she trusted me and no-one else. I felt bad telling him her secrets, but I told myself it was for the best, and I truly believe I have made the correct decisions thus far. If I hadn't told him, money wouldn't be in the process of being collected, people wouldn't be taking care of issues, etc. <BR>She is getting weaker, she is battling a cold or something, and isn't doing real well. I'm worried, but her brother says they will have the money in time for a transplant. I really hope so. Her brother was the donor for the first transplant, and when her parents found out he was going to be the donor the first time, they told her brother if he went through with it, he shouldn't plan on coming back home. Her brother went through with it, and now lives with the family friend with her. She never did anything to bring her parents down on her, she just got the luck of the draw for $hitty parents. It makes me realize how fortunate I really am to have parents who always have and always will, care for me.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I hate to say this....this is not concerning the luk.  I am assumin that she really is sick.  But the rest of the story does not sound to kosher.  Her parents wanted her to die?  They did not want her brother to help?  Her boyfriend left her becasue she was sick?  She has had this terrible life that you cannot confirm.  Just take these stories with a grain of salt.  something does not add up here............||||reg||1
Z||000015||scaredfiero||11-20-2001||07:02 AM||||She doesn't tell me these stories. I have learned most all of them through her brother. She keeps most of them inside, never to be let out, but her brother helps me understand what is really going on. She never told me she was beaten, or about boyfriends leaving her, I had no idea until her brother told me.<P>There is news, $110,000 has been raised so far, and I'm sure if something happens(she was in a coma for a week the first time)I'm sure everyone will throw more money at her. We still need to convince her to do it, but the money is almost here.||||reg||1
Z||000016||fiero56||12-06-2001||12:29 AM||||...<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 12-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||scaredfiero||12-06-2001||12:37 PM||||Its been a while since I've had a chance to update.<P>Her co-workers gave her a check for $4K the other week. She hasn't received the other money yet(at least she hasn't said anything about it yet), but I am positive she will receive it, just don't know when they plan to give it to her.<P>She has pnemonia right now, and the doctors have wanted her to go to the hospital for at least the past couple of weeks. They say she needs the transplant now, and to deal with the pnemoia, etc. She is refusing. She says she will go, but only when absolutely necessary. <P>I worry about her, but I can only do so much. She still gets very mad when I try to pressure her to go through with things. Citing I should respect her decision, etc, but then I just do stuff without her knowledge. Her brother and I are still in contact, which she doesn't know. <BR>I have a sister 2 years younger than my friend, and I thought she was the most stubborn girl I would ever know. Then I met my friend, no-one in the world is more stubborn than her, its not possible.||||reg||1
Z||000018||JSocha||12-06-2001||01:02 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by scaredfiero:<BR><B>no-one in the world is more stubborn than her, its not possible.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Obviously, you haven't met me in person.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I am really sorry to hear about your friend though and what she is going through.<P>Look at it from her possible perspective too though.  <P>How much is she suffering?  Pain?  Knowing that there is the possibility of going through it all yet again?  Feeling of obligation for those that help her?<P>Funny thing is, we will euthanize an animal so it doesn't have to suffer, even if the animal can be helped and it is perfectly okay.<P>However, as humans, we won't consider this as an option and the suffering they may be going through only to prolong it through the marvels of medicine and to have that person suffer unreasonably more in the end or in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000019||scaredfiero||12-09-2001||11:00 AM||||Latest update, and possibly some good news <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>Saturday my friend's brother sent me an e-mail. He wanted to thank me for whatever I said to or did for her the other night to make her change her mind, and decide to go with the transplant. I'm not sure what exactly made her change her mind...<P>Earlier this week I read Steve's thread(http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/004672.html) about putting things off and regretting not doing them. It reminded me of things I regret not saying or doing, and so decided to let my friend know how I really feel about her, and what I wanted, rather than regrett and wonder for the rest of my life. I told her Friday night(and she felt the same for me), and Saturday she tells her brother she wants to go to the hospital and get the transplant. I can only guess this was at least partly what helped change her mind. I haven't had a chance to talk to her since Friday, so she hasn't told me she  wants to go to the hospital yet, but I'm hoping its true. She's needed the transplant for weeks, so it will probably happen very soon if she is serious.||||reg||1
Z||000020||maryjane||12-09-2001||11:50 AM||||We are very happy to hear this latest news, for both of you. Wish you only the best and more good news in the future. Hang in there, both of you. We'll be thinking of you over the holidays.<BR>Don & Jane||||reg||1
Z||000021||Raydar||12-09-2001||06:12 PM||||Yes. Absolutely. <BR>We wish the best for her. We'll be thinking about her as well.<BR> ||||reg||14
Z||000022||scaredfiero||12-17-2001||01:15 PM||||update.<P>She decided to have the transplant performed. Her pnemonia has been getting worse, and she will be going into the hospital this friday. The doctor performing the transplant won't be available until Jan 1 so that is the day she will be getting the transplant. She will be in the hospital from this friday until at least 2 weeks after the transplant has been performed(minimum 4 weeks). The day she goes in the hospital is the same day I leave the state for a week. I feel terrible, even though she does not want me to see her in the hospital(she thinks it will be too stressing for me to see her like that), I would feel better if I were at least in the same city as her. <P>She has been having her lungs drained every few days, and I really hope she can hang on long enough to get the transplant and make it through recovery. Even the procedure scares the $hit out of me. Several extreme doses of chemo to kill all bone marrow in her body, then she is fed marrow donated by her brother through an I.V with the hopes that her body accepts the foreign marrow. If her body accepts it, the 5-year survival rate(used to gauge complete remission) is approx 58%. If her body rejects the marrow, she has no marrow and...<P>I am very scared, but glad she is going to go through with it.<P>I guess she let it go pretty long. They say she has a 25% chance of surviving the transplant. It would be lower, but they are having one of the best doctors in the country perform the procedure(hence the 2 week wait), so they say her chances are 25%.<p>[This message has been edited by scaredfiero (edited 12-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||scaredfiero||12-20-2001||09:48 AM||||I've been researching everything I can, especially this morning. I am understanding why the doctors aren't expecting much to happen. She already had one marrow transplant this spring, and the leukemia came back. I guess in the past when this has happened(particularly so soon after a BMT) the results are rarely positive. <P>She goes in the hospital early tommorrow, so we are meeting tonight to say our hopefully not last, goodbye.||||reg||1
Z||000024||scaredfiero||12-23-2001||09:27 PM||||Got a call from her brother last night. She gave everyone a good scare yesterday, but she's breathing on her own again now. We're not even beginning any of the serious stuff yet, and there have already been a few complications. She was feeling well enough to send me an email today, which was very nice. I guess beginning around Friday, she begins the serious chemo/radiation to destory all bone marrow, and the new from her brother will be injected Monday.||||reg||1
